# Database dumps -- they carry users.password_hash and api_keys.key_hash.
#
# The DIRECTORY is the mechanism. Anything inside beacon.dump/ is ignored
# whatever it is called, so filing a dump there needs no thought about
# extensions. Names inside record when the dump was TAKEN, not when it was
# filed: a dump's whole value is which state of the database it captures.
#
# The globs below are a backstop for a dump dropped in the repo root, and they
# CANNOT be complete. Nothing in this tree produces dumps -- no pg_dump target
# in the Makefile, no caller anywhere -- so every name is typed by hand, and
# pg_dump's default output format is plain SQL. `beacon.bak`, `beacon.pgdump`,
# `beacon.custom`, `beacon.tar.gz` and `beacon.sql` all slip past these and
# look exactly like the covered case in `git status`. Put dumps in the
# directory; do not rely on the suffix list growing to meet you.
#
# Bare `*.sql` is deliberately absent: this is a text-to-SQL repo, a .sql file
# is likelier to be a fixture someone means to commit, and an ignore rule that
# silently drops a file from `git add` is this same defect pointed backwards.
beacon.dump/
*.dump
*.dump.*
*.backup
*.sql.gz
